(You don't even need to say anything about "subject to the same
phenomenon"; relativity itself says that people on the train won't
find anything)

"Mass" doesn't really increase.  You can formulate things such that
inertial mass increases, but gravitational mass doesn't change.
Rather than breaking the rather nice (apparent) equality between the
two, you can alternatively leave mass alone and define a relativistic
momentum.  Personally, I like this approach.

As to measuring this effect, that's pretty easy.  Look at a
relativistic particle in an accelerator, and figure out how many
times c Newtonian mechanics wants it to be going.  Then note that
it's actually doing nothing of the sort.

> About thirty years ago a team led by G. Harry Stine performed a series of
> experiments designed to determine the speed of (presumably massless) gravity
> waves.  The conclusion was that gravity is "at least thirty thousand times
> faster than light."  

